 "stop": "20 lines",
 "poem": "The Mississippi is flowing,\nAnd the west wind whistles over\nThe red and gray fields of Unionville, and\nI love thee, Little River! Mother!\nThe Elk-Irwin Mountains rise,\nThey break their waves with thunderous roar,\nTheir crash and shriek is ringing\nThe snowfalls along the hills above,\nAlong the lake in river.\nThey are all happy together,\nFor I think of the river river,\nWhere thou art seated beneath because\nOf my morn's sunshine, sister!\nThe Blue-Eyed Rabbit whispers\nTo his little rabbit-brother near\nThe entrance to your old garden door,\nIn a dimly-illuminated room.\n\"O dear,\" says the Rabbit, \"this morning,\nWhen the sun lies high overhead on\nThe setting of summer, see",
